why 2 0 OVERLAP The tune leaps up a fourth to D and the alto leaps up to B with it, above where the soprano was. They move together, so the order stays clear.
why 2 1/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT The chord changes on this eighth: B and D over the bass's G make a G major chord, not B minor with a wrong note. The bass then walks G–E–F♯ into the cadence.
why 2 1/8 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Tenor and bass move from F♯/B to D/G a fifth apart in contrary motion. Bach accepts contrary fifths in quick eighth notes; strict exercises avoid them.
why 2 1/4 LEAPS After its leap up the bass turns back down by a third to E, then steps up to F♯. It turns, by a skip instead of a step.
why 3 3/4 CROSSING The alto steps down F♯–E–D♯ under the tenor's held F♯, so they trade places for a beat. On the downbeat the alto rises to E and the tenor falls to B.
why 3 7/8 CROSSING The alto reaches D♯, the leading tone of E, still under the tenor's F♯; both resolve on the next downbeat.
why 7 1/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs G–A–B–C♯ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 7 3/4 DOUBLING The alto leaps up to B while the tenor steps C♯–B; both land on the third of the G chord.
why 8 1/4 DOUBLING Over the bass's F♯ the tenor holds B, a 4–3 suspension that falls to A♯. The ⁶₄ doubling rule does not apply to this suspension chord.
why 8 3/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT With the bass on F♯, this eighth is V⁷ (F♯–A♯–C♯–E): the tenor's A♯ is its third, the resolution of the suspended B. The checker still hears the chord before.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence where every resolution falls: soprano C♯–B, the seventh E to D, the tenor's A♯ drops to F♯ and the bass to B.
why 9 0 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to B while the tenor resolves A♯–B, meeting in unison.
why 9 1/8 CROSSING The tenor passes down B–A–G under the bass's held B; on the next beat the bass drops to E.
why 9 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down into ii°⁶ while the bass drops a fifth. The phrase descends toward the half cadence.
why 9 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (C♯–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 11 1/2 TENDENCY The music is passing through E minor and G major here, where C♮ is a normal note. The tenor rises C–D through the chord as a plain step.
why 12 1/4 TENDENCY The bass's C is a normal note in E minor, where this cadence is. It skips to A and rises to B, the dominant of E; it has no need to fall.
why 13 1/2 SEVENTH The soprano's E is part of the A major sound held over the bass's passing F♯–E; it is not a seventh. The tune rises to F♯ as written.
why 14 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and bass move D–C♯ and G–F♯ a fifth apart, with the bass dipping to E in between. For Bach the dip breaks them up; strict exercises still count them.
why 14 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on B major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the alto's A♯ drops to F♯, the seventh E to D♯ and the bass to B.
